# Thermodynamics and Thermochemistry: Energy Bookkeeping in Chemistry

> Every chemical change either stores or releases energy, and the
> science that tracks it is thermodynamics. Why does a match release
> heat while an instant cold pack absorbs it? Why do some reactions run
> forward spontaneously while others need constant pushing? The answers
> live in three laws and one small equation, $\Delta G = \Delta H -
> T\Delta S$, the most useful inequality in chemistry.

## 1. Systems, Surroundings, and State Functions

[definition]
A **system** is the part of the universe under study; everything else
is the **surroundings**. An open system exchanges both matter and
energy, a closed system exchanges only energy, and an isolated system
exchanges nothing.
[/definition]

**State functions** — internal energy $U$, enthalpy $H$, entropy $S$,
Gibbs energy $G$ — depend only on the current state, not on the path
taken between states. Heat $q$ and work $w$ are *not* state functions:
they describe energy in transit and depend on how the change is
carried out. This path-versus-state distinction powers everything else
in the chapter.

## 2. The First Law: Energy Is Conserved

[important]
Internal energy can change only by heat flowing in or work being done:
$\Delta U = q + w$. Energy is neither created nor destroyed — it only
changes costume between heat, work, and stored chemical energy.
[/important]

@math label="eq:first-law"
\Delta U = q + w
@/math

Sign conventions matter: $q > 0$ when heat is absorbed by the system;
$w > 0$ when work is done **on** the system (compression). For
gaseous pressure-volume work against a constant external pressure:

@math label="eq:pv-work"
w = -P_{ext}\Delta V
@/math

so expansion ($\Delta V > 0$) does negative work — the system spends
energy pushing the piston.

**Special cases worth memorising:**

| Process | Condition | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Isothermal ideal gas | $\Delta T = 0$ | $\Delta U = 0$, so $q = -w$ |
| Isochoric (constant $V$) | $\Delta V = 0$ | $w = 0$, so $\Delta U = q_v$ |
| Adiabatic | $q = 0$ | $\Delta U = w$ |
| Free expansion into vacuum | $P_{ext} = 0$ | $w = 0$ |

## 3. Enthalpy: The Chemist's Energy Currency

Reactions in open vessels run at constant (atmospheric) pressure, so
chemists use **enthalpy** $H = U + PV$ instead of $U$ itself. The
change in enthalpy equals the heat absorbed at constant pressure:

@math label="eq:enthalpy"
\Delta H = q_p
@/math

- $\Delta H < 0$ — **exothermic**: heat released (combustion,
  neutralisation, respiration).
- $\Delta H > 0$ — **endothermic**: heat absorbed (photosynthesis,
  decomposition of limestone, melting ice).

The tabulated $\Delta H$ values obey **Hess's law**: because $H$ is a
state function, the enthalpy change of a reaction is the same whether
it happens in one step or many. Thermochemical cycles — most famously
the Born-Haber cycle for lattice energy — are nothing but bookkeeping
with Hess's law around a closed loop:

@math label="eq:hess"
\Delta H_{\text{overall}} = \sum \Delta H_{\text{steps}}
@/math

**Standard enthalpies** you should recognise: formation
($\Delta_f H$, of one mole from elements in standard states — zero for
an element in its reference form), combustion ($\Delta_c H$, always
negative), bond dissociation, atomisation, and solution.

## 4. Heat Capacity and Calorimetry

The heat needed to raise a temperature is $q = mc\Delta T$, with $c$
the specific heat capacity. For chemists the **molar heat capacity**
and, at constant volume or pressure, the relations below hold:

@math label="eq:heat-capacity"
q_v = nC_v\Delta T \quad\text{and}\quad q_p = nC_p\Delta T
@/math

For an ideal gas, $C_p - C_v = R$. A bomb calorimeter (constant
volume) measures $\Delta U$ directly; multiplying results for
gaseous reactants by the $\Delta n_g RT$ correction converts to
$\Delta H$:

@math label="eq:uv-h"
\Delta H = \Delta U + \Delta n_g RT
@/math

**Worked example.** Combustion of benzene in a bomb calorimeter gives
$\Delta U = -3264\ \text{kJ/mol}$:

@math label="eq:benzene"
C_6H_6(l) + \frac{15}{2}O_2(g) \to 6CO_2(g) + 3H_2O(l)
@/math

Here $\Delta n_g = 6 - 7.5 = -1.5$, so $\Delta H$ is slightly *more
negative* than $\Delta U$ by about $3.7\ \text{kJ/mol}$ — tiny against
thousands, which is why the correction often looks negligible.

## 5. The Second Law and Entropy

The first law cannot tell us why heat flows from hot to cold and never
backwards. The **second law** can: in any spontaneous process the
total entropy of the universe increases.

@math label="eq:entropy-def"
\Delta S_{\text{univ}} = \Delta S_{\text{sys}} + \Delta S_{\text{surr}} > 0
@/math

Entropy $S$ measures the number of accessible microscopic
arrangements — disorder, loosely. It is a state function with units
$\text{J K}^{-1}\text{mol}^{-1}$, and for a reversible heat exchange:

@math label="eq:entropy-rev"
\Delta S = \frac{q_{rev}}{T}
@/math

Entropy rises with melting, vaporisation, dissolution of most salts,
and whenever gas moles increase. The **third law** completes the
picture: the entropy of a perfect crystal at $0\ \text{K}$ is zero,
giving absolute entropies their reference point.

[info title="Gibbs energy, the chemist's compass"]
The second law hides one inconvenience: we usually cannot measure the
surroundings' entropy directly. Gibbs energy fixes this by folding the
surroundings into the temperature:
$\Delta S_{\text{surr}} = -\frac{\Delta H_{\text{sys}}}{T}$, so
multiplying through by $-T$ gives a criterion using system quantities
alone.
[/info]

## 6. Spontaneity: The Gibbs Energy Test

@math label="eq:gibbs"
\Delta G = \Delta H - T\Delta S
@/math

At constant $T$ and $P$, $\Delta G < 0$ means spontaneous,
$\Delta G = 0$ means equilibrium, and $\Delta G > 0$ means the reverse
direction is spontaneous. The four cases:

| $\Delta H$ | $\Delta S$ | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| $-$ | $+$ | Spontaneous at all temperatures |
| $+$ | $-$ | Never spontaneous |
| $-$ | $-$ | Spontaneous at low $T$ (freezing, below the freezing point) |
| $+$ | $+$ | Spontaneous at high $T$ (boiling, above the boiling point) |

The temperature where a sign flips is found by setting $\Delta G = 0$,
giving $T = \frac{\Delta H}{\Delta S}$ — the melting or boiling point
in disguise.

**Coupled reactions.** Even an uphill reaction ($\Delta G > 0$) can be
driven by pairing it with a strongly downhill one — how metabolism
pushes biosynthesis using ATP hydrolysis, and how graphite converts to
diamond industrially alongside favourable side reactions.

[summary]
Thermodynamics is energy accounting. The first law,
$\Delta U = q + w$, conserves energy, with $w = -P_{ext}\Delta V$ for
gas expansion. Enthalpy $\Delta H = q_p$ tracks heat at constant
pressure, and Hess's law cycles any path into standard formation and
combustion data. Calorimetry measures $q = mc\Delta T$; bomb
calorimeters give $\Delta U$, converted by $\Delta H = \Delta U +
\Delta n_g RT$. The second law demands $\Delta S_{\text{univ}} > 0$,
and Gibbs energy compresses that test into $\Delta G = \Delta H -
T\Delta S$ — negative means spontaneous, zero means equilibrium, and
the sign-flip temperature is $T = \frac{\Delta H}{\Delta S}$. Energy
decides possibility; entropy decides direction.
[/summary]
